{"content":"**Fixed — transplant markers are now machineAuthor-authenticated at parse time** (`parseTransplantMarker`, `js/common/guestTransplant.js`, round 3), following the repo's own gh-828 doctrine you cited.

One deliberate deviation from the suggested mechanism, in the trust-proof direction: the `actor` payload field alone could not secure this — the marker lives on the **guest's own PR**, so a forged `actor="<machine login>"` is as writable as the rest of the payload. The doctrine's real teeth are the comment **author**, which the guest cannot forge:

- `parseTransplantMarker(coments, trustedLogins)` now requires the machineAuthor allowlist; no allowlist / empty allowlist / a comment with no readable author → **no marker** (fail closed — a feed with only guest-authored markers reads as "no marker", so the fresh path re-arms from the human label event instead);
- author matching uses the exact `reviewVerdicts` spelling (`user.login || author.login || author.name`, lowercased, `[bot]` stripped) — same trust anchor as `readVerdictRecords`;
- the wiring (`guestMarker`) passes `verdictMachineLogins(effectiveConfig)` on every probe — the action, the sweep's orphan probe, and the `completionRentry` re-entry all flow through it (the sweep now threads `machineLogins` through `sweepClosedGuestBranches` instead of the old `[]`);
- writers additionally stamp `actor: <first machineAuthor login>` into every marker they post (arm, parked, re-arm, transplanted) for audit and human rendering.

Pinned in the round 3 suite: forged-newest-vs-real-older feed → the recorded arm sha wins (E1 intact); empty/no allowlist → null; author-less comment → null; case/`[bot]` normalization; `resolveTransplantSha` on the authenticated feed still recorded-wins.
","path":"/home/runner/work/dmtools-agents/dmtools-agents/outputs/review_replies/thread_13.md"}

{"content":"**Resolved as your option 2 — the contract is now documented as an integration contract, not overstated as in-repo enforcement** (round 3).

The wiring-the-dispatch option would have to live in the consuming project's standard validate-fresh lane — the whole gh-1140 design point is that the machine PR is indistinguishable from any same-repo machine PR ("zero fork-specific code"), so there is no guest-transplant dispatch site in this repo's wiring to hook. Within this PR, the honest fix is the one you sanctioned:

- the `guest-transplant` rule description (`sm_github.json`) now names the integration point precisely: the consuming project's validate leg fetches the dispatched workflow's job graph and calls `guestValidationDispatchPlan(jobs, smGuestTransplant.gate, workflowFile)` **before** dispatching a guest-transplant leg, failing the leg when `ok` is false — and states explicitly that **a project that skips the hook has NO gate enforcement**;
- the checker's docblock (`js/common/guestTransplant.js`) carries the same contract, and the "impossible by construction" claim is scoped to the structural transport property it actually describes (no fork-owned ref is ever targeted), no longer to the gate ordering.

Net: the ticket's "asserted by UT on the dispatched job graph" now refers to a documented project-side hook rather than an unenforced convention. If you'd rather see the checker wired into a shipped dispatch path, that is a lane-level change (the shared validate-fresh rule) and deserves its own ticket — happy to take it there.
